  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 219 PM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: High pressure today will begin to depart tonight into \ntomorrow evening with the approach of a weak, moisture starved cold \nfront for tomorrow. GOES-19 visible satellite shows this front \nnicely with a narrow band of cloud cover stretched across the \nMichigan Upper Peninsula to west central Quebec. As the front moves \ninto northern New York tomorrow morning, antecedent dry conditions \nat the surface will limit much if not all of the precipitation that \nwill be associated with the boundary. Virga looks likely with \ndewpoint depressions of 5-10 degrees ahead of the front. A few \nplaces along the northwest facing slopes of the Adirondacks and \nnorthern Greens, including Jay Peak, may see a few sprinkles, but \naccumulations will be little to non-measureable. In the deeper \nvalleys, we will likely not have enough time to saturate before dry \nair advects in behind the boundary cutting off any precipitation \nchances by tomorrow early afternoon. Winds will turn to the north-\nnorthwest as the front moves through advecting sharply colder air \nfrom Canada. Monday night lows will cool to the upper 20s for many \nlocations with a few locations hovering around the freezing mark in \nthe wider valleys. Tuesday will feature sunny skies with continued \ncool high temperatures struggling to reach the upper 40s. The \ncoldest night of the season arrives Tuesday night under strong \nradiational cooling. Overnight lows may approach some records for \nMontpelier and St. Johnsbury, with records elsewhere residing in the \n10th percentile or lower. Temperatures will drop to the teens across \nthe Adirondacks and low 20s elsewhere. Temperatures slowly rebound \nWednesday as high pressure and south winds help to moderate our flow \npattern.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: High pressure overhead will allow the air mass to slowly \nmoderate Wednesday night into next weekend with highs climbing back \ninto the upper 50s and 60s on Thursday and Friday. There is the \npotential for a few showers on Thursday night with another weak cold \nfront, about 30-60% chance of rain outside of central and south-\ncentral Vermont, which have lower chances, closer to 10-40%. The \nforecast for Halloween trick-or-treating looks dry and seasonably \nmild under high pressure with afternoon temperatures in the upper \n50s to mid 60s, falling into the lower and mid 50s in the evening.",
